nt  Morsi was held for solitary confinement  in almost almost six years that that  breaches the standards of torture under  international law and that they say the  Egyptian government is required to  investigate the reasons why former  president Morsi died there was also a  group of British MPs who formed  something called the detention review  panel which investigated the conditions  under which the new president Morsi was  being kept and they said that the lack  of medical care and inadequate medical  care for his liver disease and diabetes  meant that he was a risk of premature  death which they declared in a report  last year and so you know there are many  groups that drew a long-term attention  to the conditions under which he was  being held okay so a lot of questions  out there a lot of allegations what's  the Egyptian government saying about  them and about how he died well as you  mentioned at each public prosecutor said  in a report that they would investigate  they also said that shortly before 5  p.m.  Cairo time yesterday former president  Morsi was dead on arrival at a hospital  in Cairo and that he had died after  fainting and collapsing in the courtroom  they said that they seized the cameras  that were in the courtroom and that they  questioned those who were inside the  defendants cage with him which implies  that they are under scrutiny and they  also reportedly buried in an unknown  site to the east of Cairo early this  morning in an effort to prevent his  Braves from becoming a pilgrimage site  for his supporters ok now the the Muslim  Brotherhood that Morsi once led is  calling for protests so what can we  expect in Cairo in the days  well there is likely to be some outrage  it is you know important to remember  that this man was an extremely  controversial figure he was a symbol of  democracy and freedom for some a group  that was long underground and then came  to rule Egypt but for others he and seen  as an enemy of the state and was seen 